-sending:
-- Software: smtprelay (mail/smtprelay).
-- smtprelay does not queue. It relays each message to Fastmail SMTP
-  during the client's session, logging in with the stored
-  credentials, and answers the client's DATA with Fastmail's reply.
-  When Fastmail rejects a message, the client gets Fastmail's code
-  and text. When Fastmail cannot be reached, the client gets a 4xx
-  reply. smtprelay never accepts a message it has not handed to
-  Fastmail, and never sends a bounce.
-- smtprelay adds one Received header to each message, as every MTA
-  does. It does not rewrite the sender, any other header, or the
-  body.
-- smtprelay checks the envelope sender and each envelope recipient
-  against allowlists in its config, before it contacts Fastmail.
-  Adding an address is a one-line change to the instance's config.
-- Allowed envelope senders (MAIL FROM): =claude@p7x.dev=. Any other
-  sender is refused with a 4xx reply.
-- Allowed envelope recipients (RCPT TO):
-  - =pat@patmaddox.com=
-  - any address at =p7x.dev=
-  - any address at =lists.p7x.dev=
-- Domains match exactly and without regard to case. =p7x.dev= does
-  not match its subdomains. That is why =lists.p7x.dev= is listed on
-  its own.
-- Any other recipient is refused with a 4xx reply to its RCPT TO.
-- smtprelay refuses a sender or recipient with 451, and the code is
-  not configurable. 4xx means "try again later", which is wrong for
-  a refused address, but no client here queues or retries, so the
-  refusal still ends the send.
-- A message with any refused recipient is sent to no one. smtprelay
-  refuses the recipient; the client then abandons the message (see
-  clients).
+posting:
+- Software: OpenSMTPD (mail/opensmtpd).
+- OpenSMTPD never relays. It delivers to the list and nowhere else,
+  so nothing a client posts leaves lab-mail except as a message that
+  mbsync copies into a Fastmail folder.
+- It accepts any envelope sender.
+- It sorts each envelope recipient (RCPT TO) into one of three kinds:
+  - =lab-devel@p7x.dev= is delivered to the list.
+  - A known address is accepted and discarded: =pat@p7x.dev=,
+    =pat@patmaddox.com=, and =claude@p7x.dev=. The list is where
+    everyone reads mail for these addresses, so a reply to all lands
+    once, on the list. Adding an address is a one-line change to the
+    instance's config.
+  - Any other recipient is refused with a 5xx reply to its RCPT TO.
+- A message with any refused recipient is posted to no one. The
+  client abandons it on the refusal (see clients).
+- OpenSMTPD checks each message before it answers the client's DATA,
+  and refuses with a 5xx reply a message that is not well formed:
+  - a header line that is neither a field nor the continuation of
+    one;
+  - headers that are not ended by an empty line;
+  - anything other than exactly one From, one Subject, and one
+    Message-ID.
+  So a client that sends something other than a mail message learns
+  it while it is still connected, and nothing reaches the list.
+- A message to several recipients lands on the list once, with its
+  To and Cc unchanged. OpenSMTPD adds a Received header, Dovecot adds
+  Return-Path and Delivered-To, and nothing else is changed. Who a
+  message was addressed to is read from its To and Cc. Delivered-To
+  names =lab-devel@p7x.dev= on every message, and a Bcc leaves no
+  trace.
+- OpenSMTPD hands each message for the list to Dovecot over LMTP, on
+  a unix socket inside the jail, and Dovecot files it into =lists=.
+  OpenSMTPD never opens the maildir.
+- OpenSMTPD answers the client's DATA once the message is in its
+  queue. A delivery that fails is retried from the queue, so a message
+  posted while Dovecot is stopped lands when Dovecot is back. A
+  delivery Dovecot refuses for good is logged, and its bounce is
+  discarded like any other mail to a known address.
 
